  <!-- 1. The problem -->
  <Container first>
    <h1>Two questions about long-running agents</h1>
    <p class="text">
      Agents that run for a long time sometimes stall and sometimes fail. That raises two questions.
      First, when a run stalls, should you restart it or wait, and what in the distribution of run
      times decides? Second, how do you estimate the probability of a failure too rare for direct
      simulation to reach?
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      Two pilots on real agents are planned, one for each question, each with kill criteria
      registered before any data. The direction is still being chosen between them, and approach
      and results are held back pending that choice.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <NoteRow>
    <p class="text">
      Both questions have textbook versions: for the first, a run-time law you know and restarts
      that are independent of each other; for the second, failures that are independent across
      runs. The first then has an exact answer, and for the second the classical arithmetic shows
      why direct simulation usually sees nothing. This page works those versions out, draws them,
      and lets you vary them. Every quantitative statement
      below is about that classical arithmetic, computed and checked for this page. None of it is
      project data, and none of it describes the project's method.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="note">
      <b>Bears on:</b> survival analysis · rare-event estimation · agent evaluation.<br />
      <b>Advisor:</b> Yang Li, Computer Science.<br />
      <b>Status:</b> ongoing, Aug. 2026 – present.
    </Fragment>
  </NoteRow>

  <!-- 2. Restart or wait -->
  <Container>
    <h1>Restart or wait</h1>
    <p class="text">
      A run has been going for time <Tex src={String.raw`\tau`} /> and has not finished. Someone has
      to decide whether to kill it and start again, or keep waiting. In the textbook setting, with
      a known distribution of run times and independent restarts, that decision has an exact answer.
      Waiting costs, in expectation, the time the run still has to go: its mean residual life
      <Tex src={String.raw`m(\tau) = \mathbb{E}[T-\tau \mid T>\tau]`} />. Restarting costs a relaunch
      <Tex src="c" /> plus a fresh run, <Tex src={String.raw`c + \mathbb{E}[T]`} />. So restart if and
      only if
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`m(\tau) > \mathbb{E}[T] + c.`} />
    <p class="text">
      The same inequality decides whether a standing policy of restarting every run at cutoff
      <Tex src={String.raw`\tau`} /> beats never restarting, as the next section shows. In the
      classical setting, then, what in the distribution of run times decides is the mean residual
      life, compared with a fresh run plus the cost of a relaunch.
    </p>
  </Container>

