  <!-- 3. The classical baseline -->
  <Container>
    <h1>The classical baseline</h1>
    <p class="text">
      This section assumes a run-time law that is known exactly and restarts that are independent and
      identically distributed. Nothing is fitted to agent data. No public source of the project
      states these derivations: they were derived for this page and checked numerically (the checks
      are listed under Figure 5).
    </p>

    <h2>Notation</h2>
    <p class="text">
      <Tex src={String.raw`T \ge 0`} /> is the run time of one attempt, with CDF
      <Tex src="F" />, survival function <Tex src="S = 1 - F" />, density <Tex src="f" /> and hazard
      <Tex src="h = f/S" />. A cutoff-<Tex src={String.raw`\tau`} /> policy kills any attempt still
      running at age <Tex src={String.raw`\tau`} /> and relaunches it at cost
      <Tex src={String.raw`c \ge 0`} />, paid on each relaunch but not on the first launch.
      <Tex src={String.raw`T_{\tau,c}`} /> is the total time until the first attempt finishes under
      that policy.
    </p>

    <h2>Expected time under a cutoff</h2>
    <p class="text">Write <Tex src={String.raw`A(\tau)`} /> for the truncated mean:</p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`A(\tau) = \mathbb{E}[\min(T,\tau)] = \int_0^\tau S(t)\,dt.`} />
    <p class="text">
      The number of attempts is geometric with success probability <Tex src={String.raw`F(\tau)`} />,
      so the expected number of failed attempts is <Tex src="S/F" />, and each costs
      <Tex src={String.raw`\tau + c`} />. The attempt that succeeds costs <Tex src="T" /> given
      <Tex src={String.raw`T \le \tau`} />, and
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T\,\mathbf 1\{T\le\tau\}] = A(\tau) - \tau S(\tau)`} />. Hence
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}] = \frac{A(\tau) + c\,S(\tau)}{F(\tau)}.`} />

    <h2>An identity, and the rule</h2>
    <p class="text">The mean residual life of a run that has reached age <Tex src={String.raw`\tau`} /> is</p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`m(\tau) = \mathbb{E}[T - \tau \mid T > \tau] = \frac{\mathbb{E}[(T-\tau)^+]}{S(\tau)}.`} />
    <p class="text">
      Splitting <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T]`} /> on whether the run finishes by
      <Tex src={String.raw`\tau`} /> gives
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T] = \big(A(\tau) - \tau S(\tau)\big) + S(\tau)\big(\tau + m(\tau)\big) = A(\tau) + S(\tau)\,m(\tau).`} />
    <p class="text">
      By the previous display, <Tex src={String.raw`F(\tau)\,\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}] = A + cS`} />.
      Adding <Tex src={String.raw`S(\tau)\big(m(\tau) - c\big)`} /> gives the same quantity, so,
      whenever <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T] < \infty`} />,
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T] = F(\tau)\,\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}] + S(\tau)\,\big(m(\tau) - c\big).`} />
    <p class="text">
      So <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T]`} /> is a weighted average of
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}]`} />, with weight <Tex src={String.raw`F(\tau)`} />,
      and <Tex src={String.raw`m(\tau) - c`} />, with weight <Tex src={String.raw`S(\tau)`} />. For
      <Tex src={String.raw`0 < F(\tau) < 1`} /> and finite <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T]`} />,
      the first lies below the average exactly when the second lies above it:
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}] < \mathbb{E}[T] \iff m(\tau) > \mathbb{E}[T] + c.`} />
    <p class="text">
      The same inequality decides the one-shot question for a run stalled at age
      <Tex src={String.raw`\tau`} />: restarting once and never again costs
      <Tex src={String.raw`c + \mathbb{E}[T]`} />, and waiting costs <Tex src={String.raw`m(\tau)`} />.
    </p>

    <h2>The best cutoff</h2>
    <p class="text">Differentiating the expected time under a cutoff,</p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\frac{d}{d\tau}\,\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}] = \frac{S F - f\,(A + c)}{F^2}.`} />
    <p class="text">At an interior optimum <Tex src="A + c = SF/f" />, which gives</p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau^*,c}] = \frac{1}{h(\tau^*)} - c.`} />
    <p class="text">
      Where restarting gains almost nothing, <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}]`} /> is flat
      to machine precision, and a search on its values, such as golden-section search, cannot locate
      <Tex src={String.raw`\tau^*`} />.
      Bisection on the sign of the numerator <Tex src="SF - f(A + c)" /> still can, and that is how
      the widget below finds it.
    </p>

    <h2>Lognormal closed forms</h2>
    <p class="text">
      Let <Tex src={String.raw`T = e^{\mu + \sigma Z}`} /> with <Tex src="Z" /> standard normal,
      <Tex src={String.raw`z = (\ln\tau - \mu)/\sigma`} />, and <Tex src={String.raw`\Phi`} /> the
      standard normal CDF. Then
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`A(\tau) = e^{\mu+\sigma^2/2}\,\Phi(z-\sigma) + \tau\,\big(1-\Phi(z)\big),`} />
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[(T-\tau)^+] = e^{\mu+\sigma^2/2}\,\big(1-\Phi(z-\sigma)\big) - \tau\,\big(1-\Phi(z)\big).`} />
    <p class="text">
      <Tex src={String.raw`\Phi`} /> comes from the site's normal-tail routine (an erf series and the
      Laplace continued fraction), always evaluated on the tail that avoids cancellation.
      With median 1 (<Tex src={String.raw`\mu = 0`} />) the two main quantities take a few lines:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={CODE} lang="javascript" />
  </Container>

  <Container tone="gray">
    <div class="ar-svg" set:html={ruleSvg} />
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 2.</b> What in the distribution decides it, classically: the mean residual life.
      Lognormal run time with <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 1.5`} /> and relaunch cost
      <Tex src="c = 0.5" />, in multiples of the median. Restarting at cutoff
      <Tex src={String.raw`\tau`} /> beats never restarting exactly where
      <Tex src={String.raw`m(\tau)`} /> exceeds <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T] + c`} />. The two
      crossings fall at the same <Tex src={String.raw`\tau_0 = 0.309`} />, because
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T] = F(\tau)\,\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}] + S(\tau)\,(m(\tau) - c)`} />.
      Best cutoff <Tex src={String.raw`\tau^* = 1.56`} />, where expected time is 1.85 against 3.08.
      Classical baseline, not project data.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <NoteRow>
    <p class="text">
      Read Figure 2 from left to right. Before <Tex src={String.raw`\tau_0`} />, a run that has gone
      <Tex src={String.raw`\tau`} /> is expected to finish sooner than a relaunch plus a fresh run, so a
      cutoff there only adds time. Past <Tex src={String.raw`\tau_0`} />, every cutoff in the shaded
      region beats never restarting, and the best of them is <Tex src={String.raw`\tau^*`} />.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="note">
      Why both crossings share <Tex src={String.raw`\tau_0`} />: with weights
      <Tex src="F" /> and <Tex src="S" />, <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T]`} /> averages
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}]`} /> and <Tex src="m - c" />, so one equals
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T]`} /> exactly when the other does.
    </Fragment>
  </NoteRow>

  <!-- 4. How much restarting buys -->
  <Container>
    <h1>How much restarting buys</h1>
    <p class="text">
      The rule says when restarting helps; how much it helps depends on the shape of the law.
      Figure 3 holds the lognormal family at median 1 and varies <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma`} />,
      with free relaunches on one side of the slider and a relaunch cost of half a median run time
      on the other. Drag the divider to compare. Each curve is
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}]/\mathbb{E}[T]`} />, and the dots mark the best cutoffs.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container tone="gray">
    <img-comparison-slider class="slider-container">
      <figure slot="first" class="before">
        <div class="ar-svg ar-cmp" set:html={lnC0Svg} />
        <figcaption>c = 0</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure slot="second" class="after">
        <div class="ar-svg ar-cmp" set:html={lnC05Svg} />
        <figcaption>c = 0.5</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </img-comparison-slider>
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 3.</b> Classical baseline, not project data. Lognormal run times with median 1.
      With free restarts (left), restarts help high-variance runs enormously: at
      <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 2`} /> the best cutoff, about a twentieth of the median, cuts
      expected time below a tenth. They help low-variance runs essentially not at all: at
      <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 0.5`} /> the gain is 3 parts in 10⁸. With a relaunch cost of
      half a median run time (right), every best cutoff moves later and every gain shrinks:
      <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 2`} /> now saves three quarters (ratio 0.248), not nine tenths.
      Exact closed form, checked against quadrature and, at <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 2`} />, a
      seeded simulation.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <p class="text">
      A lognormal is one shape among many. Figure 4 has two others, chosen to make a point rather than
      fitted to anything: a law with two modes, and runs that can hang forever.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container tone="gray">
    <Slides count={2} labels={['Two-mode law', 'Runs that hang']}>
      <div class="slide-content" style="display: block;">
        <div class="ar-svg" set:html={mixSvg} />
      </div>
      <div class="slide-content" style="display: none;">
        <div class="ar-svg" set:html={hangSvg} />
      </div>
    </Slides>
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 4.</b> Classical baseline, not project data: two illustrative laws, not fitted to
      any agent (use the dots to switch).
      <b>Two-mode:</b> 80% of runs are fast (median 1, <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 0.5`} />) and a
      fifth are slow (median <Tex src="e^3" />, about 20, <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 0.5`} />), at
      relaunch costs <Tex src="c = 0" />, 0.25 and 1. With free relaunches, killing any run still
      going at about 1.8 fast medians cuts expected time from 5.46 to 1.72.
      <b>Runs that hang:</b> each run hangs forever with probability <Tex src="q" /> = 0.01, 0.05 or
      0.2, and finishing runs are lognormal with <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 1`} />. Never
      restarting then takes infinitely long, so some cutoff is mandatory. With free relaunches the
      best cutoff stays between about 1.1 and 1.7 medians as <Tex src="q" /> goes from 0.2 to 0.01.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- 5. Interactive -->
  <Container>
    <h1>Restart or wait, interactively</h1>
    <p class="text">
      Figure 5 computes the classical quantities for a law you choose:
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T]`} />, <Tex src={String.raw`F(\tau)`} />,
      <Tex src={String.raw`m(\tau)`} />, <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}]`} />, whether
      restarting at <Tex src={String.raw`\tau`} /> helps, and the best cutoff
      <Tex src={String.raw`\tau^*`} />. Drag across the plot or use the sliders. Beside the exact
      value of <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}]`} />, a seeded simulation of the restart
      process runs to 200,000 replications, and its running mean, with one standard error, converges
      onto the exact value. At cutoffs so early that one replication would need too many attempts to
      run live, the widget says so instead.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      Two cases need care. If runs can hang, <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T]`} /> and
      <Tex src={String.raw`m(\tau)`} /> are infinite, the identity and the rule do not apply, and any
      finite cutoff beats never restarting. And where the best attainable gain is below
      <Tex src="10^{-6}" /> of <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T]`} />, <Tex src={String.raw`\tau^*`} />
      is numerically ill-defined, so the widget reports that restarting gains nothing measurable
      instead of printing one.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="extra-large" tone="gray">
    <AgentRestartWidget id="restart-widget" />
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 5.</b> Classical baseline, not project data: a known run-time law and independent
      restarts. Exact values come from the closed forms above, with <Tex src={String.raw`\Phi`} /> from
      the site's normal tail; <Tex src={String.raw`\tau^*`} /> comes from bisection on the sign of
      <Tex src="SF - f(A+c)" />. The simulation is seeded, so the same settings give the same digits.
      The defaults are the case of Figure 2, <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 1.5`} /> and
      <Tex src="c = 0.5" />, where <Tex src={String.raw`\tau^* = 1.563`} /> and
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau^*,c}] = 1.853`} /> against
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T] = 3.080`} />. The two-mode law keeps
      <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 0.5`} /> for both modes with the fast median at 1, so its times
      are in fast medians; the hanging law keeps finishing runs lognormal with median 1 and
      <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 1`} />.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <h2>Reference values</h2>
    <p class="text">
      The lognormal family with median 1, at the cutoffs that minimise expected time. At
      <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 0.5`} /> the best cutoff is ill-conditioned (Checks, below), so
      the table reports the gain instead.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead class="center">
          <tr>
            <th><Tex src={String.raw`\sigma`} /></th>
            <th><Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T]`} /></th>
            <th><Tex src="c" /></th>
            <th><Tex src={String.raw`\tau^*`} /> (medians)</th>
            <th><Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau^*,c}]\,/\,\mathbb{E}[T]`} /></th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="center">
          {ROWS.map((r) => (
            <tr><td>{r.s}</td><td>{r.et}</td><td>{r.c}</td><td>{r.star}</td><td>{r.ratio}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="caption">
      Classical baseline (known lognormal run-time law, independent restarts); not project data.
      Every row was also reproduced by an independent implementation.
    </p>

    <h2>Checks</h2>
    <p class="text">
      These are checks of this page's classical arithmetic only, not of the project. The closed
      forms were checked numerically before publication, against pass criteria recorded in a
      configuration file as fixed before the first run. Of the 69 registered checks, 68 passed and
      one failed.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Check</th><th>Cases</th><th>Worst observed</th><th>Result</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Closed form against numerical quadrature</td><td>9 laws × 6 cutoffs</td><td>4.5 × 10⁻¹⁵ relative</td><td>pass</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The identity <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T] = F\,\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}] + S\,(m - c)`} /></td><td>6 finite-mean laws × 3 values of <Tex src="c" />, 8001-point grid</td><td>5.9 × 10⁻¹⁶ relative</td><td>pass</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The sign rule <Tex src={String.raw`\mathbb{E}[T_{\tau,c}] < \mathbb{E}[T] \iff m(\tau) > \mathbb{E}[T] + c`} /></td><td>18 cases</td><td>0 sign mismatches</td><td>pass</td></tr>
          <tr><td>First-order condition at <Tex src={String.raw`\tau^*`} /></td><td>20 cases</td><td>1.06 × 10⁻⁴ against a 10⁻⁵ criterion (<Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 0.5`} />, <Tex src="c = 0.5" />)</td><td>19 pass, 1 fail</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Seeded simulation of the restart process, 200,000 replications per case</td><td>5 cases</td><td>|z| = 2.76 against a criterion of 4</td><td>pass</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text">
      The failure is one of solver conditioning, not a counterexample. At
      <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 0.5`} /> the objective is flat to machine precision, so
      golden-section search cannot locate <Tex src={String.raw`\tau^*`} />; bisection on the numerator
      <Tex src="SF - f(A+c)" /> satisfies the condition to 7.8 × 10⁻¹⁶, and an independent bisection
      agrees, with <Tex src={String.raw`\tau^* = 20.13`} /> and a gain of 8.0 × 10⁻¹¹. That diagnosis
      was made after the fact and is not a registered check; the registered failure stands as
      recorded. The <Tex src={String.raw`\sigma = 0.5`} />, <Tex src="c = 0" /> check passes only
      narrowly (5.8 × 10⁻⁶ against 10⁻⁵), for the same reason. Also after the fact, 10 further seeds
      for the simulation case with the largest <Tex src="|z|" /> gave a mean <Tex src="z" /> of
      −0.031. Every reference value on this page was reproduced independently in Python, with
      <code>math.erfc</code> and bisection.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- 6. Rare failures -->
  <Container>
    <h1>Failures too rare to see</h1>
    <p class="text">
      The second question: how do you estimate the probability of a failure too rare for direct
      simulation to reach? Picture an agent run as a loop of model and tool calls that, rarely, ends
      in failure. With per-run failure probability <Tex src="p" /> and <Tex src="N" /> independent
      runs, the number of failures is <Tex src={String.raw`K \sim \mathrm{Bin}(N, p)`} />, and the
      classical arithmetic is short. The chance of seeing no failure at all is
    </p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\Pr(K = 0) = (1-p)^N \approx e^{-Np},`} />
    <p class="text">the relative standard error of the estimate <Tex src={String.raw`\hat p = K/N`} /> is</p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`\frac{\mathrm{sd}(\hat p)}{p} = \sqrt{\frac{1-p}{Np}},`} />
    <p class="text">and when <Tex src="K = 0" /> the one-sided upper bound is</p>
    <Tex block src={String.raw`p_{\mathrm{U}} = 1 - \alpha^{1/N} \approx \frac{-\ln \alpha}{N} \approx \frac{3}{N} \quad\text{at } \alpha = 0.05.`} />
    <p class="text">
      So when failures are much rarer than one in the number of runs you can afford, direct
      simulation usually sees none, and zero failures in <Tex src="N" /> runs supports only
      <Tex src="p" /> below about <Tex src="3/N" />: for <Tex src="N = 1000" />,
      <Tex src="p < 0.00299" /> at 95%. Crude Monte Carlo then stops being an estimator: it returns
      zero occurrences and no information about the magnitude.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="large" tone="gray">
    <div class="ar-svg" set:html={rareSvg} />
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 6.</b> Question 2. When failures are much rarer than one in the number of runs you
      can afford, direct simulation usually sees none, and zero failures in <Tex src="N" /> runs
      supports only <Tex src="p" /> below about <Tex src="3/N" />. Drawing a failure as reachable
      from every call is illustrative. This is the problem only; this project's approach is held
      back.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <p class="text">
      Figure 7 does that arithmetic for <Tex src="p" /> from <Tex src="10^{-12}" /> to 0.1 and
      <Tex src="N" /> from 10 to <Tex src="10^7" />, with a seeded direct
      simulation beside the exact value: whole experiments of <Tex src="N" /> runs, counting how many
      saw no failure. Push <Tex src="p" /> well below <Tex src="1/N" /> and nearly every experiment
      comes back empty.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="extra-large" tone="gray">
    <AgentRareCount id="rare-widget" />
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 7.</b> Classical arithmetic, not project data. The curve is the exact chance of no
      failure in <Tex src="N" /> runs, <Tex src="(1-p)^N" />, evaluated as
      <Tex src={String.raw`\exp(N\,\mathrm{log1p}(-p))`} />; the hollow dot is the fraction of seeded
      simulated experiments that saw none, with one binomial standard error at the exact value. The bound after zero failures is
      evaluated as <Tex src={String.raw`-\mathrm{expm1}(\ln(0.05)/N)`} />. At the defaults,
      <Tex src="p = 0.001" /> and <Tex src="N = 1000" />, the exact chance of seeing nothing is 0.3677.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- 7. What we are doing -->
  <Container>
    <h1>What we are doing</h1>
    <p class="text">
      This is a research project in Advanced Topics in Artificial Intelligence (COMS 6720),
      Department of Computer Science, Iowa State University, advised by Prof. Yang Li. We are asking
      the two questions above of real tool-using LLM agents: when an agent stalls, when is restarting
      it worth more than waiting, and how can the probability of a failure too rare for direct
      simulation be estimated?
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      Two pre-registered pilots on real agents are planned, one on restart policies and one on rare
      failures, each with its kill criteria registered before any data. The direction is still being
      chosen between them.
    </p>
  </Container>

